## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to design end-to-end tests for offline-first collaborative applications—a common pattern in modern productivity tools. It evaluates understanding of multi-client synchronization, conflict resolution strategies, and proper test isolation.

## Key Concepts
- **Offline-first syncing and eventual consistency**: Understanding how data propagates when clients reconnect
- **Conflict resolution strategies**: CRDT (Conflict-free Replicated Data Types) ensures deterministic merging; last-writer-wins uses timestamps
- **Multi-client test isolation**: Using separate browser contexts to simulate concurrent users
- **Network simulation**: Leveraging tools like Playwright's network throttling to emulate offline/online transitions
- **Per-note permissions and presence**: Validating access control and real-time user indicators

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright setup with two isolated contexts
const { chromium } = require('playwright');

const browser = await chromium.launch();
const context1 = await browser.newContext();
const context2 = await browser.newContext();

const user1 = await context1.newPage();
const user2 = await context2.newPage();

// Seed shared note
const noteId = await createSharedNote(user1, 'Test Note');
await user2.goto(`/notes/${noteId}`);

// Take user1 offline
await context1.setOffline(true);

// User1 edits offline
await user1.fill('.editor', 'User1 offline edit');

// User2 edits online
await user2.fill('.editor', 'User2 online edit');

// Restore connectivity
await context1.setOffline(false);

// Verify sync and conflict resolution
const finalContent = await user1.textContent('.editor');
console.log('Final content:', finalContent);
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for real-time collaboration with CRDTs are complex due to concurrent edits and network churn. This question probes end-to-end orchestration, deterministic testing, and conflict-resolution validation.

## Key Concepts
- CRDT-based synchronization across two clients
- WebSocket messaging and presence propagation
- Offline edits and merge semantics
- Test determinism via mocked timelines and seeded data

## Code Example
```javascript
const { test, chromium } = require('@playwright/test');

test('collab doc CRDT merge with offline edits', async ({ browser }) => {
  const ctxA = await browser.newContext();
  const ctxB = await browser.newContext();

  const pageA = await ctxA.newPage();
  const pageB = await ctxB.newPage();

  await pageA.goto('/doc/seeded-doc-id');
  await pageB.goto('/doc/seeded-doc-id');

  // Verify presence
  await expect(pageA.locator('.user-list')).toContainText('User B');
  await expect(pageB.locator('.user-list')).toContainText('User A');

  // Concurrent edits
  await pageA.locator('.editor').type('Hello ');
  await pageB.locator('.editor').type('World');

  // Verify CRDT merge (order may vary based on timestamps)
  await expect(pageA.locator('.editor')).toHaveText(/Hello World/);

  // Simulate disconnect
  await pageB.evaluate(() => window.mockWebSocket.close());
  await pageB.locator('.editor').type(' from offline');

  // Reconnect and verify merge
  await pageB.evaluate(() => window.mockWebSocket.reconnect());
  await expect(pageA.locator('.editor')).toHaveText(/Hello World from offline/);

  // Verify no duplicate edits
  const textA = await pageA.locator('.editor').textContent();
  const textB = await pageB.locator('.editor').textContent();
  expect(textA).toBe(textB);
});
```

**Answer: Playwright** — superior isolation with contexts, better mocking capabilities via browser automation, and more reliable for multi-tab/session scenarios.

